sprog_glossary_subpage = Glossary
sprog_glossary_heading = Glossary
sprog_glossary_lead = Fixed term translations per language pair — so technical terms and proper nouns are translated the same way everywhere.
sprog_glossary_help_summary = When should I use the glossary?
sprog_glossary_help_idea = A glossary defines: whenever a given term appears in the source language, it is always translated the same way. It does not translate by itself — it enforces consistency for individual technical terms.
sprog_glossary_help_examples_label = Typical examples
sprog_glossary_help_example_1 = Product and brand names that should never be translated (e.g. „Sprog" → „Sprog").
sprog_glossary_help_example_2 = Jargon with exactly one correct translation (e.g. „Geschäftsführung" → „Management Board").
sprog_glossary_help_example_3 = Tone-of-voice choices a machine would otherwise guess wrong (e.g. „Mitarbeitende" → „team members", not „employees").
sprog_glossary_help_when_label = Good for
sprog_glossary_help_when_1 = Single words or short phrases that must be translated identically everywhere.
sprog_glossary_help_when_2 = Terms an AI or a new translator would otherwise vary.
sprog_glossary_help_notwhen_label = Not for
sprog_glossary_help_notwhen_1 = Fixed text blocks shown in the frontend — those are what wildcards are for.
sprog_glossary_help_notwhen_2 = Whole sentences or running text — those are translated in the editor.
sprog_glossary_help_notwhen_3 = Terms that differ by context — a glossary always enforces the same translation.
sprog_glossary_help_status = Note: the automatic link to machine translation and the editor is still in preparation. Entries are already saved, but do not take effect yet.
